15 0 obj
<< /A 56 0 R /Alt (Flowchart diagram illustrating a Perceptual Graph Kernel-based plant phenotyping framework, with sequential steps including plant image acquisition, image preprocessing, phenotypic trait extraction, perceptual trait encoding, trait graph construction, computation, and resulting in classification and phenotyping output. Each step lists specific sub-processes or features.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 39 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
16 0 obj
<< /A 57 0 R /Alt (Four-panel graphic depicting plant leaf image processing steps: Panel A is a natural RGB leaf image with background. Panel B shows the same leaf with background removed and clear boundaries. Panel C displays a color-transformed leaf highlighting greenness variation. Panel D presents a grayscale texture map emphasizing surface irregularities and leaf vein structure.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 40 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 58 0 R /Alt (Panel \(a\) shows a healthy tomato plant with dense green leaves and clusters of ripening tomatoes in red, orange, and green. Panel \(b\) depicts a tomato plant under moderate stress, with yellowing leaves and mostly green or yellowish tomatoes. Panel \(c\) presents a plant under severe stress, characterized by extensive browning and wilting of leaves, sparse foliage, and orange tomatoes.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 43 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 59 0 R /Alt (Graph diagram showing five labeled circles: Height, Curl, Leaf Colour, Leaf Size, and Soil. Arrows connect circles with numbers zero, one, or two, representing relationships or weights between the variables.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 44 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
19 0 obj
<< /A 60 0 R /Alt (Side-by-side diagram illustrating two network graphs: Panel A shows healthy plant trait interactions with dense, interconnected, and symmetrical green lines between nodes labeled leaf area, mean intensity, shape irregularity, texture energy, and texture contrast. Panel B shows stressed plant trait interactions with fewer, thinner, and asymmetric lines between the same nodes, indicating fragmented and disrupted relationships. Explanatory text below describes strong coordination in healthy plants versus reduced coordination and disrupted relationships in stressed plants.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 44 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
20 0 obj
<< /A 61 0 R /Alt (Three network diagrams compare trait interactions under healthy, mild stress, and severe stress plant conditions. Each diagram shows nodes for traits like leaf size, color, soil moisture, and curl, with node size representing trait perceptual intensity and edge thickness representing trait interaction strength. Healthy condition nodes and edges are green and distributed evenly, mild stress is orange with slightly thicker edges and varied node sizes, and severe stress is dark red with the thickest edges and highest node intensity. A legend clarifies color, node, and edge definitions.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 46 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
21 0 obj
<< /A 62 0 R /Alt (Box plot comparing PGK similarity scores across three zone health categories: Healthy Zones \(highest median, green\), Mild Stress Zones \(moderate median, orange\), and Severe Stress Zones \(lowest median, red\), illustrating decreasing similarity with increasing stress.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 47 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
22 0 obj
<< /A 63 0 R /Alt (Bar chart comparing average PGK similarity scores across ten spatial zones for Acre 1, Acre 2, and Acre 3, showing higher scores in zones 1 to 4 and notable declines in zones 7 and 9.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 47 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
23 0 obj
<< /A 64 0 R /Alt (Three photographs at the top show organized rows of plants in farm fields bordered by trees and mountains under a cloudy sky. Below, three color-coded tables labeled Acre 1, Acre 2, and Acre 3 detail grid plots Z1 to Z10, each with highlighted yellow circles representing sample points P1, P2, and P3. Each acre\220s grid visually displays the zone layout and indicates where representative plants were sampled. A legend explains that yellow dots mark sample points.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 48 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
24 0 obj
<< /A 65 0 R /Alt (Matrix chart showing ten zones \(Z1 to Z10\) for Acre 1, Acre 2, and Acre 3, with zone health status color-coded: green for healthy, orange for mild stress, and red for severe stress, based on PGK similarity.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 49 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
25 0 obj
<< /A 66 0 R /Alt (Flowchart illustrating the process of analyzing tomato plant health using images showing healthy, mildly stressed, and severely stressed plants, extracting traits like leaf color, size, curl, and height, building perceptual trait interaction graphs, and visualizing interactions via network diagrams and a heatmap indicating interaction intensity from low \(blue\) to high \(red\).) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 50 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
26 0 obj
<< /A 67 0 R /Alt (Three photographs labeled Field A, Field B, and Field C show decreasing plant health from dense green vegetation in Field A to yellow-brown in Field C. Below, three corresponding pixelated health maps labeled Health Map A, B, and C display increasing yellow, orange, and red zones, indicating moderate to severe plant stress. A color key defines green for healthy, yellow for moderate stress, orange for high stress, and red for severe stress.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 50 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
27 0 obj
<< /A 68 0 R /Alt (Three side-by-side heatmap visualizations of confusion matrices for a PGK model applied to Acre 1, Acre 2, and Acre 3, each showing classification counts for healthy and stressed labels, with yellow indicating higher values and a colorbar legend on the right of each chart.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 51 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
28 0 obj
<< /A 69 0 R /Alt (Three-panel graphic with corresponding network diagrams below: panel \(a\) shows lush green healthy plants with a green network, \(b\) presents mildly yellowing plants with a yellow network, and \(c\) displays severely yellowed plants with a red network, indicating stress progression.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 51 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
